\section{What mimic protects against}{

The package is built for an \strong{honest researcher who makes mistakes}:
printing rows, saving identifiers, releasing small cells, saving a fitted
model that carries its data, suppressing a cell that the total gives
away, or releasing a table that can be subtracted from last month's.
Against these it is thorough, and when it cannot tell, it does not
approve.

It does \strong{not} protect against a researcher who deliberately smuggles
data out. A script can hide values in what it is allowed to release: in
the shape of a figure, in the last digit of a rounded number, in which
line it fails, or in a word chosen from its own code for the error
message. One bit per run is enough over many runs. No automatic check
can rule that out. What helps is outside the package:
\itemize{
\item agreements and trust: access only for known researchers under contract,
\item staff who read the script, not only the output,
\item a limit on the number of runs and on what each run may release,
\item file system permissions and no network in the secure environment.
The write guard in \code{\link[=real_run]{real_run()}} is a safety net, not a sandbox.
}

Two further limits: figures and Office files are always reviewed by
hand, and a level 4 copy from \code{\link[=mimic]{mimic()}} is built from the real data and
is output like any other.
}

\section{Principles}{

\itemize{
\item Data protection comes first. When in doubt the package chooses the safe
option and warns.
\item No real rows ever leave the secure environment: not in the copy, the
description, error messages or logs.
\item Synthesis methods are implemented in the package itself rather than
borrowed, so they can be tuned for small populations over time.
}
